NAME

reed - Edit UTF-8 text files

SYNOPSIS

    reed [FILE]
    reed --help

DESCRIPTION

Reed is a modal text editor. With no file argument it opens an unnamed buffer.
A missing file is created only when it is saved successfully. Editing uses the
alternate screen; leaving Reed restores the terminal screen.

Normal mode navigates and edits the document. Insert mode enters text.
Selection mode highlights text or complete lines for editing and clipboard
operations. Command mode accepts file operations. Search mode keeps literal
matches highlighted while navigating them.

OPTIONS

    -h, --help — Show concise invocation help and exit.

KEYBINDINGS

Movement

    h — Move one character left (Normal).
    Left — Move one character left (Normal and Insert).
    j — Move down one visual row (Normal).
    Down — Move down one visual row (Normal and Insert).
    k — Move up one visual row (Normal).
    Up — Move up one visual row (Normal and Insert).
    l — Move one character right (Normal).
    Right — Move one character right (Normal and Insert).
    b — Move backward to the end of the previous word (Normal).
    B — Move backward to the beginning of the current or previous word (Normal).
    n — Move forward to the end of the current or next word (Normal).
    N — Move forward to the beginning of the next word (Normal).
    a — Move to the current logical line start (Normal).
    Home — Move to the logical line start (Normal and Insert).
    e — Move to the current logical line end (Normal).
    End — Move to the logical line end (Normal and Insert).
    A — Move to line start and enter Insert mode.
    E — Move to line end and enter Insert mode.
    g — Move to the document start (Normal).
    Ctrl+Home — Move to the document start (Normal).
    G — Move to the document end (Normal).
    Ctrl+End — Move to the document end (Normal).
    [ — Move to the previous paragraph (Normal).
    ] — Move to the next paragraph (Normal).
    Page Up — Move up one viewport (Normal).
    Page Down — Move down one viewport (Normal).

Editing

    i — Enter Insert mode at the cursor (Normal).
    o — Open a line below the current line (Normal).
    O — Open a line above the current line (Normal).
    Enter — Insert a newline (Insert).
    Escape — Return to Normal mode (Insert).
    d — Delete at the cursor (Normal).
    Backspace — Delete before the cursor (Normal and Insert).
    Delete — Delete at the cursor (Normal and Insert).
    u — Undo (Normal).
    U — Redo (Normal).

Selection and clipboard

    s — Start character selection (Normal); cancel or change to characters (Selection).
    S — Start line selection (Normal); cancel or change to lines (Selection).
    Alt+S — Select the whole document (Normal).
    Escape — Cancel selection (Selection).
    d — Delete the selected text (Selection).
    c — Copy selected text (Selection).
    x — Copy, then delete selected text (Selection).
    v — Replace the selection with clipboard text (Selection).
    c — Start a copy motion or whole-line operation (Normal).
    x — Start a cut motion or whole-line operation (Normal).
    v — Paste clipboard text at the cursor (Normal).

Search

    / — Open command entry (Normal).
    ? — Open incremental search (Normal).
    Backspace — Delete the previous query character (Search prompt).
    Delete — Delete the query character at the cursor (Search prompt).
    Enter — Move to the nearest match after accepting the query (Search prompt).
    Escape — Cancel search and restore its starting cursor (Search prompt).
    n — Move to the next match without wrapping (Search mode).
    b — Move to the previous match without wrapping (Search mode).
    ? — Exit Search mode at the current cursor.
    Escape — Exit Search mode at the current cursor.

Word motions treat ASCII letters, digits, underscore, and non-ASCII Unicode
scalars as word characters; punctuation and whitespace separate words.
Paragraphs are runs of nonblank logical lines separated by blank lines. In
wrapped mode vertical movement follows visual rows; line and paragraph
commands use logical lines.

CONFIGURATION

Reed reads `~/.config/reed/config` once at startup. The file is optional and
is not created automatically. Built-in defaults are:

    wrap=true
    tab_width=4

`wrap` accepts `true` or `false`. `tab_width` accepts an integer from 1 through
16. Blank lines and whitespace around assignments are accepted. Lines may
contain `#`, `;`, or `//` comments. Duplicate or unknown keys, invalid values,
malformed input, or a read failure reject the complete file. Reed keeps both
built-in defaults and displays the path and specific error in its status row;
parse errors include a line number. The file is never rewritten. Settings are
not reloaded during an editing session.

COMMANDS

    /s — Save the current file.
    /q — Quit with unsaved-change protection.
    /sq — Save and quit only after a successful save.
    /saveas PATH — Save to a new path; an existing destination is not replaced.
    /open PATH — Open another file after applying the save/discard prompt.
    /find TEXT — Find literal text forward, wrapping to document start.

Use Tab to complete a command name. Command names are case-insensitive and
must otherwise match exactly. Failed commands leave the document open.

EXAMPLES

    reed
    reed notes.txt

NOTES

Reed stores valid UTF-8 and rejects NUL or malformed UTF-8 when opening a
file. Cursor movement and display are scalar-based; grapheme editing, wide
character layout, and combining-mark layout are not supported. Saving an
existing file checks that it has not changed since opening. A conflict must
be resolved in Reed; Save As does not overwrite. Failed or uncertain saves
leave edits in the buffer. Undo and redo retain grouped changes in memory and
are limited by available process memory.

